Lab 5: Documenting Ansible Playbooks
Objectives

By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

    Implement comprehensive documentation strategies for Ansible playbooks
    Add meaningful comments and descriptions to playbook components
    Create clear explanations for tasks, roles, and variables
    Apply industry best practices for playbook documentation
    Produce role-level README documentation that supports maintainability in team environments

Prerequisites

Before starting this lab, students should have:

    Basic understanding of Ansible concepts (playbooks, tasks, roles, variables)
    Familiarity with YAML syntax and structure
    Experience writing basic Ansible playbooks
    Knowledge of Linux command line operations

Ansible must be installed in your lab environment. If it is not already present, install it with:

sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y ansible

or, on RHEL/CentOS-based systems:

sudo yum install -y ansible

Verify the installation:

ansible --version

You should see version output similar to ansible [core 2.14.x] or later.
Lab Environment

This lab uses a self-contained local project directory â�� no remote hosts are required. All tasks run against the local Ansible controller using localhost, so you can complete the entire lab safely in an isolated practice environment.

Create your working directory now:

mkdir -p ~/ansible-doc-lab
cd ~/ansible-doc-lab

Task 1: Build a Documented Inventory and Variables File
Step 1.1: Create a documented inventory

Create the project structure:

mkdir -p documentation-lab/{playbooks,roles,group_vars,inventory,templates}
cd documentation-lab

Create an inventory file with header comments explaining ownership and purpose:

cat > inventory/hosts.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Lab Inventory File
# =============================================================================
# Purpose: Local practice inventory for the documentation lab.
# Maintainer: Infrastructure Automation Team (student exercise)
# Notes: All tasks target localhost so the lab is safe to run without
#        provisioning real remote servers.
# =============================================================================

all:
  children:
    webservers:
      hosts:
        localhost:
          ansible_connection: local
      vars:
        # Web server specific variables consumed by group_vars/webservers.yml
        http_port: 80
        https_port: 443
        document_root: /tmp/ansible-doc-lab/www
EOF

Step 1.2: Create a documented group_vars file

cat > group_vars/webservers.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Web Servers Group Variables
# =============================================================================
# Purpose: Configuration variables for all hosts in the webservers group.
# Author: Infrastructure Automation Team (student exercise)
# Dependencies: Consumed by playbooks/web-server-setup.yml
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web Server Configuration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Primary web service configuration.
# NOTE: this lab does not install a real web server package; the service
# name is documented here for reference only and is not started.
web_service:
  name: "apache2"                 # Service name (apache2 on Debian/Ubuntu, httpd on RHEL)
  state: "stopped"                # Kept stopped in this lab - no package is installed
  enabled: false                  # Not enabled at boot for this exercise

# HTTP/HTTPS port configuration used by the sample playbook and templates
ports:
  http: 80                        # Standard HTTP port
  https: 443                      # Standard HTTPS port

# Document root and web content settings
web_content:
  document_root: "/tmp/ansible-doc-lab/www"   # Local sandbox directory, not a system path
  index_file: "index.html"                    # Default index file name

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security Configuration (documented for reference; not applied in this lab)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Firewall rules that a production playbook would apply
firewall_rules:
  - port: "{{ ports.http }}"
    protocol: "tcp"
    comment: "Allow HTTP traffic"
  - port: "{{ ports.https }}"
    protocol: "tcp"
    comment: "Allow HTTPS traffic"
EOF

Deliverable: inventory/hosts.yml and group_vars/webservers.yml exist and both use header comment blocks describing purpose, author, and dependencies. Verify with:

ansible-inventory -i inventory/hosts.yml --list

Expected result: a JSON structure showing the webservers group with localhost as a member and the http_port/https_port/document_root variables present under _meta or group vars output, with no YAML syntax errors reported.
Task 2: Create a Fully Documented Playbook
Step 2.1: Write the main playbook

Create playbooks/web-server-setup.yml. This playbook is intentionally scoped to safe, local actions (creating a sandbox directory and rendering a template) so it can run without root privileges or a real web server installed.

cat > playbooks/web-server-setup.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Web Server Sandbox Setup Playbook
# =============================================================================
#
# PLAYBOOK INFORMATION:
# Name: web-server-setup.yml
# Purpose: Demonstrate documentation best practices using a safe, local
#          sandbox that creates a document root and renders an index page.
# Author: Infrastructure Automation Team (student exercise)
# Version: 1.0.0
#
# DESCRIPTION:
# This playbook performs the following steps:
# - Displays host facts relevant to the configuration
# - Creates a local sandbox document root directory
# - Renders a documented Jinja2 template into that directory
# - Reports a summary of what was configured
#
# REQUIREMENTS:
# - Runs against localhost using the "local" connection plugin
# - No sudo privileges required
# - No external network access required
#
# USAGE:
# ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/web-server-setup.yml
#
# TAGS AVAILABLE:
# - facts:   Display host information
# - setup:   Directory creation tasks
# - content: Template rendering tasks
# - report:  Summary reporting tasks
# =============================================================================

- name: "Documented Web Server Sandbox Setup"
  hosts: webservers
  become: false
  gather_facts: true

  pre_tasks:
    - name: "FACTS | Display target host information"
      debug:
        msg: |
          Configuring sandbox on: {{ inventory_hostname }}
          Operating System: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}
          Architecture: {{ ansible_architecture }}
      tags: ['facts']

  tasks:
    - name: "SETUP | Create local sandbox document root"
      file:
        path: "{{ web_content.document_root }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      tags: ['setup']

    - name: "CONTENT | Render documented index page from template"
      template:
        src: "index.html.j2"
        dest: "{{ web_content.document_root }}/{{ web_content.index_file }}"
        mode: '0644'
      tags: ['content']

  post_tasks:
    - name: "REPORT | Display configuration summary"
      debug:
        msg: |
          Sandbox Configuration Complete!
          ================================
          Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
          Document Root: {{ web_content.document_root }}
          Index File: {{ web_content.index_file }}
          Service Name (reference only): {{ web_service.name }}
      tags: ['report']
EOF

Step 2.2: Create the supporting documented template

cat > templates/index.html.j2 << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
    Template: index.html.j2
    Purpose: Default landing page rendered by web-server-setup.yml
    Generated: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
    Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>{{ inventory_hostname }} - Ansible Documentation Lab</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to {{ inventory_hostname }}</h1>
    <ul>
        <li>Operating System: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}</li>
        <li>Document Root: {{ web_content.document_root }}</li>
        <li>Rendered: {{ ansible_date_time.date }} {{ ansible_date_time.time }}</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Deployed via Ansible for documentation practice.</em></p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

Step 2.3: Run and validate the playbook

Check the syntax first, then run it:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/web-server-setup.yml --syntax-check
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/web-server-setup.yml

Deliverable: The playbook run completes with failed=0 in the final PLAY RECAP line, and the rendered file exists:

cat /tmp/ansible-doc-lab/www/index.html

Expected result: valid HTML output showing the host's distribution name, document root, and a rendered timestamp â�� confirming the documented template variables resolved correctly.
Task 3: Create and Document an Ansible Role
Step 3.1: Scaffold the role

ansible-galaxy init roles/webserver

This creates the standard role skeleton (tasks/, defaults/, handlers/, meta/, templates/, vars/, files/, tests/).
Step 3.2: Document the role's tasks and defaults

cat > roles/webserver/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Web Server Role - Main Tasks
# =============================================================================
# Role: webserver
# File: tasks/main.yml
# Purpose: Entry point for the role. Creates a documented sandbox web root
#          and reports the resulting configuration. Intentionally avoids
#          package installation so the role is safe to run anywhere.
# =============================================================================

- name: "WEBSERVER | Ensure sandbox document root exists"
  file:
    path: "{{ webserver_document_root }}"
    state: directory
    mode: '0755'
  tags: ['webserver', 'setup']

- name: "WEBSERVER | Render sandbox index page"
  template:
    src: "index.html.j2"
    dest: "{{ webserver_document_root }}/index.html"
    mode: '0644'
  tags: ['webserver', 'content']

- name: "WEBSERVER | Report role configuration"
  debug:
    msg: |
      Role 'webserver' applied to {{ inventory_hostname }}
      Document root: {{ webserver_document_root }}
      Service name (reference only): {{ webserver_service_name }}
  tags: ['webserver', 'report']
EOF

cat > roles/webserver/defaults/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Web Server Role - Default Variables
# =============================================================================
# Role: webserver
# File: defaults/main.yml
# Purpose: Sensible defaults for the role. Override in group_vars, host_vars,
#          or playbook vars as needed.
# =============================================================================

# Local sandbox document root used by tasks/main.yml
webserver_document_root: "/tmp/ansible-doc-lab/role-www"

# Reference-only service name (not started by this role)
webserver_service_name: "apache2"

# Standard ports documented for future expansion of this role
webserver_ports:
  http: 80
  https: 443
EOF

cat > roles/webserver/templates/index.html.j2 << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
    Template: index.html.j2
    Purpose: Sandbox index page rendered by the webserver role
    Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>{{ inventory_hostname }} - webserver role</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>webserver role output</h1>
    <p>Document root: {{ webserver_document_root }}</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

cat > roles/webserver/meta/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
# =============================================================================
# Web Server Role - Metadata
# =============================================================================
galaxy_info:
  role_name: webserver
  author: Infrastructure Automation Team (student exercise)
  description: Sandbox role demonstrating documentation best practices for Ansible roles
  license: MIT
  min_ansible_version: 2.9
  platforms:
    - name: Ubuntu
      versions:
        - 20.04
        - 22.04
    - name: EL
      versions:
        - 8
        - 9

dependencies: []
EOF

Step 3.3: Create Role Documentation

Create a complete README.md for the role describing its purpose, variables, and usage:

cat > roles/webserver/README.md << 'EOF'
# Web Server Role (Sandbox)

## Overview

This Ansible role demonstrates documentation best practices for a reusable
role. It creates a local sandbox document root and renders a documented
Jinja2 template into it. It intentionally does not install real web server
packages or manage system services, making it safe to run repeatedly in any
practice environment.

## Requirements

- Ansible 2.9 or later
- No special privileges required (role does not use `become`)
- No external network access required

## Role Variables

Defaults are defined in `defaults/main.yml`:

| Variable                  | Default                          | Description                                  |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `webserver_document_root` | `/tmp/ansible-doc-lab/role-www`  | Local sandbox directory rendered content goes into |
| `webserver_service_name`  | `apache2`                        | Reference-only value; not started by this role |
| `webserver_ports.http`    | `80`                              | Documented HTTP port for future expansion     |
| `webserver_ports.https`   | `443`                             | Documented HTTPS port for future expansion    |

## Dependencies

None. This role has no dependencies on other roles or collections.

## Example Playbook

```yaml
---
- name: "Apply webserver sandbox role"
  hosts: webservers
  become: false
  roles:
    - role: webserver
      vars:
        webserver_document_root: "/tmp/ansible-doc-lab/custom-www"

Testing This Role

