EOF

Run the syntax check and then the playbook, exactly as documented in the role's README:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/test-webserver-role.yml --syntax-check
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts.yml playbooks/test-webserver-role.yml
cat /tmp/ansible-doc-lab/role-www/index.html

Deliverable: roles/webserver/README.md exists with Overview, Requirements, Role Variables, Dependencies, Example Playbook, Testing, License, and Author Information sections. The test playbook run reports failed=0 in the PLAY RECAP, and role-www/index.html contains the rendered hostname and document root path, confirming the README's documented behavior matches the actual role output.
Expected Outcomes

    A documentation-lab project directory containing a documented inventory, group_vars file, playbook, and templates that all run successfully with failed=0
    A fully scaffolded and documented webserver role whose README accurately describes its variables, usage, and test procedure
    Working familiarity with header-comment conventions, inline task descriptions, and README structure for Ansible roles

Troubleshooting

Issue 1: ansible-playbook reports "ERROR! the field 'hosts' has an invalid value" Diagnostic hint: check that inventory/hosts.yml is passed correctly with -i and that the group name in the playbook's hosts: key matches the group name in the inventory file exactly (case-sensitive).

Issue 2: Template rendering fails with "'web_content' is undefined" or similar variable errors Diagnostic hint: confirm you are running the playbook from inside the documentation-lab directory so Ansible's default group_vars/ relative path resolves, and check ansible-inventory -i inventory/hosts.yml --list to confirm the variable is actually attached to the webservers group.
Conclusion

In this lab, you built a small but complete Ansible project demonstrating documentation best practices at every level of the automation stack: an annotated inventory and group variables file, a fully commented playbook with tagged tasks and pre/post-task reporting, and a self-contained role with defaults, tasks, a template, and a complete README describing its variables, dependencies, and test procedure. You verified each artifact by actually running it, rather than just writing comments, which reinforced the principle that good documentation must stay synchronized with working code. Going forward, consider exploring ansible-doc, molecule for role testing, and automated linting with ansible-lint to further enforce documentation and quality standards in larger automation codebases.
